    id: 'labex-rust-unpacking-options-with',
    title: 'Unpacking Options with ?',
    theory: ['In this lab, we explore the use of the ? operator in Rust, which allows for easy unpacking of Option values without the need for nested match statements. The ? operator can be used to quickly return the underlying value if the Option is Some, or terminate the function and return None if the Option is None. This operator can be chained together to make code more readable and concise. Note: If the lab does not specify a file name, you can use any file name you want. For example, you can use main.rs, compile and run it with rustc main.rs && ./main. You can unpack Options by using match statements, but it\'s often easier to use the ? operator. If x is an Option, then evaluating x? will return the underlying value if x is Some, otherwise it will terminate whatever function is being executed and return None. Source: rust-lang/rust-by-example · MIT.'],
