    theory: ['In this lab, we learn about unit testing in Rust. Unit tests are Rust functions that verify the non-test code by performing setup, running the code, and asserting the results. These tests are written in a tests mod with the #[cfg(test)] attribute and marked with the #[test] attribute. Tests can fail if something in the test function panics, and helper macros like assert!, assert_eq!, and assert_ne! are used for assertions. Rust 2018 allows unit tests to return Result<()> to use the ? operator for concise testing. There is also support for testing panics using the #[should_panic] attribute. Specific tests can be run using the test name with the cargo test command, and tests can be ignored using the #[ignore] attribute or by running cargo test -- --ignored. Note: If the lab does not specify a file name, you can use any file name you want. For example, you can use main.rs, compile and run… Source: rust-lang/rust-by-example · MIT.'],
